[38;5;231m⏺[39m I haven't made the change yet. The code doesn't support "internal anyway," so I wanted to check with you first.

  - [1mNothing in the code keeps this route internal.[0m [38;5;153mserver.js[39m calls [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)[39m with no host, so
    Express listens on every network interface. Nothing checks IP addresses, and there's no internal-only router.
    Whether it's really internal depends entirely on how it's deployed (firewall, VPC, ingress rules), and I can't see
    any of that from here.
  - [1mThe route returns financial data.[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m serves revenue figures. It's the one route the code deliberately
    protects, while [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m and [38;5;153m/health[39m are left open.
  - [1mIf you remove the check[0m, anyone who can reach the port can read that data without logging in. That includes anything
    that gets exposed by accident later, like a new ingress rule or a port-forward.

  [1mOption I'd suggest:[0m a service token for the script, which is still about one line on the route:

  [32m// auth.js[39m
  [34mfunction[39m [33mrequireLoginOrToken[39m(req, res, next) {
    [34mif[39m (process.env.REPORTS_TOKEN && req.headers.authorization === [31m`Bearer [39m${process.env.REPORTS_TOKEN}`) [34mreturn[39m [33mnext[39m();
    [34mreturn[39m [33mrequireLogin[39m(req, res, next);
  }

  Then use [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrToken[39m on [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m. The dashboard team adds an [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer …[39m header to their
  script, and logged-in users keep working as before.

  (Separately, [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m only checks that a cookie named [38;5;153msession[39m exists. It doesn't verify the signature, so it's
  weaker than its comment says. That's worth fixing at some point, but it doesn't change the choice here.)

  Should I add the token option? If you're sure the deployment keeps this port off any untrusted network and you still
